1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,749
A bowl of milk with a spoon of curd.

2
00:00:02,749 --> 00:00:04,489
On a warm night, it sets.

3
00:00:04,489 --> 00:00:06,317
In the fridge, it does not.

4
00:00:06,717 --> 00:00:08,307
Same bowl, same curd.

5
00:00:08,307 --> 00:00:10,187
Only the temperature changed.

6
00:00:10,637 --> 00:00:12,075
Aarav wanted the numbers.

7
00:00:12,075 --> 00:00:14,414
He asked: why does cold slow the change?

8
00:00:14,414 --> 00:00:16,892
And why can too much
heat ruin it for good?

9
00:00:17,242 --> 00:00:18,865
Here is the short answer.

10
00:00:18,865 --> 00:00:20,722
The change is run by enzymes.

11
00:00:20,722 --> 00:00:23,219
Each enzyme works best at one temperature.

12
00:00:23,219 --> 00:00:24,563
Cold only pauses it.

13
00:00:24,563 --> 00:00:26,600
Too much heat destroys its shape.

14
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:30,957
By the end, you will know what changes
enzyme activity, the six classes

15
00:00:30,957 --> 00:00:33,501
of enzymes, and the
helpers called cofactors.

16
00:00:34,151 --> 00:00:36,391
First, what can change
an enzyme's activity?

17
00:00:36,741 --> 00:00:40,327
Anything that changes the enzyme's
3D shape, its tertiary structure.

18
00:00:40,777 --> 00:00:42,414
There are four main factors.

19
00:00:42,414 --> 00:00:43,314
Temperature.

20
00:00:43,179 --> 00:00:45,513
pH, which tells how acidic a liquid is.

21
00:00:45,513 --> 00:00:46,933
The amount of substrate.

22
00:00:46,933 --> 00:00:49,477
And special chemicals
that bind to the enzyme.

23
00:00:50,127 --> 00:00:52,072
Let's start with temperature and pH.

24
00:00:52,422 --> 00:00:56,740
Each enzyme has its highest activity
at one temperature and one pH.

25
00:00:56,740 --> 00:00:59,918
These are its optimum
temperature and optimum pH.

26
00:00:59,918 --> 00:01:02,847
Activity falls on both
sides of the optimum.

27
00:01:03,297 --> 00:01:07,086
Low temperature keeps the enzyme
in a temporarily inactive state.

28
00:01:07,086 --> 00:01:11,430
High temperature destroys its activity,
because heat denatures the protein.

29
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:13,898
Denature means the folded shape is lost.

30
00:01:14,548 --> 00:01:15,573
Remember this.

31
00:01:15,573 --> 00:01:17,394
Cold only pauses an enzyme.

32
00:01:17,394 --> 00:01:18,621
Heat destroys it.

33
00:01:19,221 --> 00:01:22,308
Now, what happens when we
add more and more substrate?

34
00:01:22,658 --> 00:01:25,083
At first, the reaction gets faster.

35
00:01:25,083 --> 00:01:29,194
Then it reaches a top speed,
called V max, and stops rising.

36
00:01:29,644 --> 00:01:30,544
Why?

37
00:01:30,082 --> 00:01:33,202
There are fewer enzyme molecules
than substrate molecules.

38
00:01:33,202 --> 00:01:37,096
Once every enzyme is busy, it is
saturated, and extra substrate has to

39
00:01:37,096 --> 00:01:37,996
wait.

40
00:01:38,227 --> 00:01:40,315
Some chemicals switch an enzyme off.

41
00:01:40,315 --> 00:01:41,866
This is called inhibition.

42
00:01:42,216 --> 00:01:45,585
A competitive inhibitor looks
very much like the substrate.

43
00:01:45,585 --> 00:01:49,621
It takes the substrate's place in the
active site, so the real substrate

44
00:01:49,621 --> 00:01:50,521
cannot bind.

45
00:01:50,944 --> 00:01:55,371
For example, malonate looks like
succinate, and blocks the enzyme succinic

46
00:01:55,371 --> 00:01:56,341
dehydrogenase.

47
00:01:56,341 --> 00:01:59,933
Such inhibitors are often used
to control harmful bacteria.

48
00:02:00,533 --> 00:02:01,433
Your turn.

49
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:03,908
Why does malonate block
succinic dehydrogenase?

50
00:02:03,908 --> 00:02:05,036
Pause and decide.

51
00:02:08,236 --> 00:02:12,356
Because it looks like succinate and
takes its place in the active site.

52
00:02:12,356 --> 00:02:14,200
It is a competitive inhibitor.

53
00:02:14,850 --> 00:02:16,880
Enzymes are sorted into six classes.

54
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:18,657
Here they are, step by step.

55
00:02:19,007 --> 00:02:21,674
Oxidoreductases move hydrogen or
electrons from one substrate to another.

56
00:02:21,674 --> 00:02:23,161
One gets oxidised, one gets reduced.

57
00:02:23,911 --> 00:02:28,139
Transferases move a group, other than
hydrogen, from one substrate to another.

58
00:02:28,889 --> 00:02:30,978
Hydrolases break bonds using water.

59
00:02:30,978 --> 00:02:34,321
Lyases remove groups without
water, leaving double bonds.

60
00:02:35,071 --> 00:02:39,444
Isomerases rearrange a molecule into
another form with the same atoms.

61
00:02:40,194 --> 00:02:42,105
Ligases join two compounds together.

62
00:02:42,105 --> 00:02:45,146
Each class has subclasses,
named with a four-digit number.

63
00:02:45,896 --> 00:02:47,996
Many enzymes also need a helper.

64
00:02:47,996 --> 00:02:49,023
Let's zoom in.

65
00:02:49,373 --> 00:02:52,516
The protein part of the enzyme
is called the apoenzyme.

66
00:02:52,516 --> 00:02:54,620
The non-protein helper is a cofactor.

67
00:02:54,620 --> 00:02:57,038
Remove it, and the enzyme stops working.

68
00:02:57,538 --> 00:03:00,709
Zoom to the active site
of catalase or peroxidase.

69
00:03:00,709 --> 00:03:03,435
A group called haem is bound tightly here.

70
00:03:03,435 --> 00:03:06,750
A tightly bound organic
helper is a prosthetic group.

71
00:03:07,250 --> 00:03:09,671
Zoom to another enzyme, carboxypeptidase.

72
00:03:09,671 --> 00:03:13,841
Its helper is a metal ion, zinc,
holding on to both the enzyme and the

73
00:03:13,841 --> 00:03:14,741
substrate.

74
00:03:15,217 --> 00:03:16,576
Now, a worked example.

75
00:03:16,576 --> 00:03:18,594
Sort three cofactors into their types.

76
00:03:18,944 --> 00:03:23,071
Haem, in catalase and peroxidase,
is organic and tightly bound.

77
00:03:23,071 --> 00:03:24,840
It is a prosthetic group.

78
00:03:25,290 --> 00:03:29,612
NAD is organic, but it joins the
enzyme only for a short time.

79
00:03:29,612 --> 00:03:30,981
It is a co-enzyme.

80
00:03:30,981 --> 00:03:33,709
NAD and NADP contain the vitamin niacin.

81
00:03:34,159 --> 00:03:36,307
Zinc, in carboxypeptidase, is a metal.

82
00:03:36,307 --> 00:03:37,861
It is a metal ion cofactor.

83
00:03:38,511 --> 00:03:39,973
Now, the exam trap.

84
00:03:40,323 --> 00:03:43,988
Many students think low temperature destroys
enzymes, just like high temperature.

85
00:03:44,388 --> 00:03:47,447
But low temperature only makes
them temporarily inactive.

86
00:03:47,447 --> 00:03:49,984
Only high temperature
denatures them for good.

87
00:03:50,634 --> 00:03:51,918
Where is this asked?

88
00:03:51,918 --> 00:03:55,677
As match questions on enzyme classes
and cofactors, and graph questions

89
00:03:55,677 --> 00:03:57,539
on temperature, pH and V max.

90
00:03:58,139 --> 00:03:59,360
Exam level now.

91
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:01,967
Match each enzyme class with its job.

92
00:04:01,967 --> 00:04:03,239
Pause and solve.

93
00:04:08,639 --> 00:04:10,114
The answer is B.

94
00:04:10,114 --> 00:04:12,503
One-s, two-p, three-q, four-r.

95
00:04:13,003 --> 00:04:14,657
Oxidoreductases move
hydrogen or electrons.

96
00:04:14,657 --> 00:04:15,754
Transferases move a group.

97
00:04:16,254 --> 00:04:18,038
Hydrolases break bonds with water.

98
00:04:18,038 --> 00:04:19,704
And ligases join two compounds.

99
00:04:20,304 --> 00:04:21,845
Here is your recap.

100
00:04:21,845 --> 00:04:23,429
Screenshot this card.

101
00:04:26,629 --> 00:04:28,815
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

102
00:04:29,215 --> 00:04:30,115
One.

103
00:04:29,684 --> 00:04:32,581
Why does a reaction stop
speeding up at V max?

104
00:04:33,481 --> 00:04:34,381
Two.

105
00:04:33,918 --> 00:04:35,762
What is a competitive inhibitor?

106
00:04:36,662 --> 00:04:37,562
Three.

107
00:04:37,170 --> 00:04:39,995
How is a prosthetic group
different from a co-enzyme?

108
00:04:41,095 --> 00:04:44,589
Next time: a cut on your knee
heals with brand new cells.

109
00:04:44,589 --> 00:04:46,426
How does one cell become two?

110
00:04:46,426 --> 00:04:49,190
We find out in the cell
cycle and its phases.
